{"remedy_type":"claude-md","routing_rationale":"The failure mode across all five incidents is the same: the agent had an explicit, previously-stated constraint from the user (no-network, work on a specific branch, don't touch the formatter, don't touch tests) and either deviated from it or silently reinterpreted the plan without flagging the conflict. This isn't heterogeneous — every case is "explicit constraint stated earlier in the conversation got dropped." A hook can't generalize here because the constraints are ad hoc and stated in natural language mid-conversation (branch names, "leave the formatter alone", "don't touch tests", "no network") — there's no fixed file pattern or tool-call signature a PreToolUse hook could match against. It's also not procedural/multi-step enough to warrant a skill; it's a single standing behavioral rule: track and honor explicit constraints stated in-session, and check actions against them before proceeding. That makes claude-md the cheapest correct remedy. Because R4 already appears to cover "acting in the wrong branch/environment" via a claude-md rule, and that is a subset of this same failure mode (explicit constraint disregarded), this proposal revises/broadens R4 rather than sitting beside it as a sibling.","failure_statement":"When the user states an explicit constraint mid-task (branch to use, no-network requirement, a file/component not to touch, scope boundaries like "don't touch tests"), the agent sometimes proceeds to violate that constraint or silently changes the plan without re-confirming, requiring the user to interrupt and re-state the constraint.","remedy_content":{"target":"global","title":"Honor explicit constraints stated in-session","body":"# Honor Explicit Constraints

When the user states an explicit constraint during a task — a branch to work on, a no-network requirement, a file/component not to modify, a scope boundary (e.g. "don't touch tests") — treat it as binding for the rest of the task, not just the turn it was said in.

Before editing a file, running a command, or otherwise acting, check the action against every constraint the user has stated so far in the conversation. If a planned action would conflict with one, stop and flag the conflict to the user instead of proceeding, silently reinterpreting the plan, or assuming the constraint no longer applies. This includes constraints about which branch/environment to work in, not just file-level restrictions."},"evidence":[{"incident_id":1,"quote":"You ignored the no-network constraint; stop changing the plan."},{"incident_id":2,"quote":"You are not following the branch instruction in /work/fake-aurora."},{"incident_id":4,"quote":"I told you to leave the formatter alone, and it was changed."},{"incident_id":5,"quote":"The fake vendor error says 'what the hell'; quote it verbatim in the fixture."},{"incident_id":8,"quote":"Follow the instructions: this patch must not touch tests."}],"dedup":[{"existing":"R1","verdict":"clear","reason":"R1 (fake-verify-before-claim) governs verifying completion claims before reporting work done. These incidents are about ignoring explicit stated constraints (branch, network, file scope) during the task, not about unverified completion claims."},{"existing":"R2","verdict":"clear","reason":"R2 (Fake Release Discipline) requires running a declared verification command before reporting a release complete — a post-hoc reporting-accuracy rule. These incidents are about mid-task constraint violations, unrelated to release verification."},{"existing":"R3","verdict":"clear","reason":"R3 is a pending hook proposal gating destructive deletions with pre-execution confirmation. These incidents aren't about destructive deletions; they're about violating already-stated constraints (branch, network, protected files/scope) during otherwise normal edits."},{"existing":"R4","verdict":"overlap","reason":"R4 is a pending claude-md proposal specifically about acting in the wrong branch/environment. That is a subset of this cluster's failure mode — incidents 1/2 are exactly a branch/environment instruction being ignored, and incidents 4/5/8 show the same underlying pattern (explicit constraint disregarded) extended to protected files and test scope. This proposal broadens R4's rule rather than standing beside it."}],"overlap_action":{"revises":"R4"},"split":[],"confidence":0.78}
